"""Arranged takes for the scores a personal pack records (BUILDING.md, "Your own games").

It is the engine tools/pack-studio runs for its AI, ENG, SID and AY takes (on its workspace's W/all);
its commands below also work on their own, for experiments. fmzx-pack personal writes
DIR/arrange/<game>/<key>/score.toml for every score it records (DIR: W/all for the studio).
This tool adds a bass, a pad (chords, one note at a time) and a drum to each score, as several
takes to choose from by ear; the next build (tools/pack-studio --build) packs the chosen takes. A
score.toml with `shadow = true` is a shadow score: music a learned or built-in routine already
plays live. Its takes are made like any score's; it is packed only when a take is chosen for it,
and the take then replaces the live version. A score can also take its game's own tune read
exactly from the game's music engine (engine, below), its game's tune from the game's C64
version, from a SID collection the owner has (chip, below), or from its Spectrum 128 version,
from a collection of .ay files the owner has (ay, below).

  tools/arrange-music generate DIR [--takes N] [--game ID]... [--force] [--jobs J]
                                   [--model NAME] [--soundfont PATH] [--seed S] [--offline]
      For each score without takes (every score with --force; only the games named by --game),
      writes N takes (default 4) to DIR/arrange/<game>/<key>/take-<n>/:
        arranged.toml  the take as the FM-1 plays it: the score's header, take, generator, a
                       [metrics] table, and notes on 4 voices: 0 lead (the score's melody: its
                       voice with role "lead", else its highest voice on average), 1 bass,
                       2 pad, 3 drums (period_t 0, level the hit's strength)
        full.mid       the original with everything the generator wrote
        fm1.mid        arranged.toml as MIDI (the drum's level picks kick, snare, clap or hat)
        full.wav, fm1.wav, and ../original.wav: renders with FluidSynth, when it is installed
                       (the default soundfont is the first .sf2 of Homebrew's fluid-synth;
                       --soundfont overrides), loudness-matched with ffmpeg when it is there
      --model picks the generator: "amt" (default), the Anticipatory Music Transformer
      (stanford-crfm/music-medium-800k) in three passes, drums, then bass, then chords, each
      hearing the original and the passes before it; "rules", a quick generator without a model
      (a bass on the melody's chord roots, a triad pad and a plain drum pattern). Takes differ by
      seed (and, for amt, top_p); a seed (--seed, default 1) gives the same takes again on the
      same machine. amt writes from 1 to about 50 notes a second of music per pass, depending
      on the take, and takes roughly 0.1 s a note on Apple silicon, so a minute of music is several minutes a take: it prints its
      progress and an estimate. --jobs runs that many scores at once (each loads the model).
      The first amt run downloads the model (about 1.4 GB) to the Hugging Face cache.
      --offline fetches nothing: only the cached model (it stops when the model is not cached;
      run once without --offline to download it). The tool runs through uv, which fetches its
      packages on the first run; UV_OFFLINE=1 keeps uv offline too (tools/pack-studio
      --offline sets both).
      A score has its takes when takes.toml next to it lists takes 1..N for its score.toml as it
      is now, whatever generator made them (--force replaces them; a changed score.toml gets new
      ones); a run interrupted with the same generator and seed resumes at the missing takes.

  tools/arrange-music engine DIR [--game ID]... [--force] [--fmzx-pack PATH] [--soundfont PATH]
      For each score whose game runs a music engine the reader knows (ntropic, Tritone, Stocker),
      reads its music exactly from the engine and writes it as DIR/arrange/<game>/<key>/engine-1/.
      fmzx-pack engine-notes (the converter of chip, below) plays the score's own run, the one
      that recorded it, as music-preview plays it: the engine found by its code, its channels'
      state read at its row read, its drums at its drum code's OUTs; the notes and drum hits on
      the score's timeline (cached in DIR/arrange/.engine-notes/ while the score, the converted
      games and fmzx-pack are the same). The take:
        arranged.toml  the score's header (key, intro_t, loop_t: the take is on the score's
                       timeline and plays in step with the beeper, as an AI take), take =
                       "engine-1", generator = "engine <engine>", source ("<engine>, read
                       exactly: <what was read>"), engine, channels, channel_roles (each
                       channel's voice), score_digest (score.toml's, as takes.toml's), [metrics]
                       notes a second per voice, notes and drum_hits (what the reader gave), and
                       the notes on 4 voices. Each channel keeps one voice for the whole take: the
                       highest on average the lead; with two channels the other the bass when it
                       lies under E3 (MIDI 52), else the pad; with three or more the lowest the
                       bass and the rest the pad (with more than one there, a newer note cuts the
                       one sounding: the pad follows the latest onset). Levels: lead 200, bass
                       210, pad 150, times the note's level / 255 (an engine's volume or duty),
                       at least 48. A same-pitch note right after another on a voice ends 128 T
                       early, so the FM voice strikes it again (within 1/32 of a period it would
                       glide on). The drum hits on voice 3 by kind (kick 200, snare 170, tom 130,
                       hat 80, an engine's tone burst 200, its noise 120), one at a time within
                       the drum rules (generate's): a hit within 40 ms of the one before merged
                       into it, the stronger kept
        fm1.mid, fm1.wav  the notes on General MIDI instruments, rendered as the takes are
      A score with no known engine gets no take (said: "no engine take: no known engine in its
      run"), nor does a shadow score a built-in entry of the firmware already plays exactly from
      the engine's state (said: "already exact, no engine take": the live entry follows the game
      as it plays and costs the pack nothing, where a take would only repeat it). A score has its
      engine take while engine-1/arranged.toml's score_digest is its score.toml's, or the cached
      reading says it needs none; --force writes it again. generate, chip and ay leave engine-<n>/
      alone. The reader is --fmzx-pack PATH, else $FMZX_PACK, else host/target/release/fmzx-pack.

  tools/arrange-music chip DIR --sid-dir PATH [--game ID]... [--max N] [--force]
                               [--fmzx-pack PATH] [--soundfont PATH]
      For each score without chip takes (every score with --force; only the games named by
      --game), looks for its game's C64 tune in the SID collection at PATH (HVSC's C64Music, or
      any folder of .sid files; nothing is downloaded) and writes up to N takes (default 3) to
      DIR/arrange/<game>/<key>/chip-<n>/, best first.
      The collection is indexed once to DIR/arrange/.chip-index.json (again when the folder's or
      STIL's modification time changes, or with --force): each .sid's path, its PSID/RSID header
      (title, author, released, songs, start song) and its STIL entries (DOCUMENTS/STIL.txt: the
      file's and each subtune's TITLE, NAME, ARTIST, AUTHOR, COMMENT).
      Candidates: files whose file name or header title is the game's name (the score's name or
      its id; words compared without case, punctuation, "the", with roman numerals as digits;
      title match 1.0, or 0.6 when one name runs on from the other), or whose STIL entry names it
      (0.8; 1.0 when the entry also mentions the Spectrum, as "[from the Spectrum game]" does).
      At most 12 (file, subtune) pairs a score are checked, a subtune of each file in turn (one
      STIL names first, then the start song, then the rest).
      Each is converted for 180 s with `fmzx-pack chip-notes` (cached in
      DIR/arrange/.chip-notes/) and checked for closeness to the score's tune (judge, below:
      about 1.0 for the same tune in another key, tempo or arrangement, under 0.2 for another
      tune). Rank: closeness + 0.1 title_match + 0.1 for a STIL entry naming the Spectrum. The
      best N are written, each as:
        arranged.toml  the score's header, voices, roles and notes as a take's (the converter's
                       voices: 0 the lead, the highest SID voice on average; 1 the bass, the
                       lowest; 2 the pad; 3 the drum), with take = "chip-<n>", generator = "chip sid", timeline = "own"
                       (the notes keep the chip tune's own timeline: own_intro_t, own_loop_t, from
                       the converter's loop, own_loop_t 0 when none was found, and one pass of the
                       loop; the score's intro_t and loop_t stay the fingerprint's), source
                       ("C64: <title> by <author> (<released>), subtune N, <file>"), sid_file,
                       subtune, stil (the STIL text that named the game, if any), and [metrics]
                       closeness, title_match, rank, stil_spectrum and notes a second
        fm1.mid, fm1.wav  the notes on General MIDI instruments, rendered as the takes are
        chip.wav       the C64's own sound, when sidplayfp is on PATH (else skipped, with a note)
      The converter is --fmzx-pack PATH, else $FMZX_PACK, else host/target/release/fmzx-pack
      (cd host && cargo build --release -p fmzx-pack). generate never touches chip-<n>/, and
      generate --force leaves them alone. generate, chip and ay end by judging the scores they
      worked on (judge, below, without --apply), so judge.toml is there for tools/pack-studio.

  tools/arrange-music ay DIR --ay-dir PATH [--game ID]... [--max N] [--force]
                             [--fmzx-pack PATH] [--soundfont PATH]
      chip's counterpart for the game's Spectrum 128 tune: for each score without ay takes, looks
      in the collection of ZXAYEMUL .ay files at PATH (Project AY's music/ay, with games/ and
      misc/, or any folder of .ay files; nothing is downloaded) and writes up to N takes (default
      3) to DIR/arrange/<game>/<key>/ay-<n>/, best first.
      The collection is indexed once to DIR/arrange/.ay-index.json (again when the folder's
      modification time changes, or with --force): each .ay's path and its header's author and
      misc strings, songs, first song and the songs' names.
      Candidates: files whose file name or misc string is the game's name, or one of whose songs'
      names is (compared as chip compares them; title match 1.0, or 0.6 when one name runs on
      from the other); at a tie, a file under games/ first, then one under misc/. At most 12 (file,
      song) pairs a score are checked, a song of each file in turn (one named for the game first,
      then the first song, then the rest). Songs the converter refuses (48K beeper tunes,
      Amstrad CPC and SAM songs, files that need a special player) are skipped without a line
      each and counted. The closeness check, the rank (without STIL) and the takes are chip's, with
      take = "ay-<n>", generator = "chip ay", source ("Spectrum 128: <title> by <author>, song N,
      <file>": the song's name, else the file's misc string), ay_file and subtune (the song,
      1-based). ay.wav, the Spectrum 128's own sound, is written when ffmpeg reads .ay files
      (its libgme demuxer), else skipped with a note. chip and generate leave ay-<n>/ alone, and
      ay leaves chip-<n>/ and take-<n>/ alone.

  tools/arrange-music pick DIR [--port P] [--set GAME/KEY=N]... [--list]
      Serves a page on http://127.0.0.1:P/ (default 8765, or a free port) to listen to each
      score's original, its takes and its engine, chip and ay takes and choose one, saved to
      DIR/arrange/choices.toml:
          [choices]
          "<game>/<key>" = <take>     # 0 or absent: the original
          "<game>/<key>" = "engine-1" # an engine take, by its folder
          "<game>/<key>" = "chip-1"   # a chip take, by its folder
          "<game>/<key>" = "ay-1"     # an ay take, by its folder
      --set writes choices without the page (N, engine-N, chip-N or ay-N); --list prints every
      score, the judge's verdict, its takes, their metrics, its engine, chip and ay takes with
      their source, and
      each take's judge figures (closeness, quality, its place in the judge's order, its faults),
      and the choice ("(the judge)" after one the judge made). The page shows the same. Choices
      of other scores in the file are kept, and a choice made here is the owner's (the judge's
      mark on it goes). Before the list or the page it judges every score (judge, below, without
      --apply; quick once judge.toml is there) and warns of each faulty take: "warning:
      <game>/<key> chip-2: refused by the renderer: ...", "plays only a fragment: ...". The page
      and the list mark a shadow score "(replaces the live version when chosen)"; its original is
      the live version.

  tools/arrange-music judge DIR [--game ID]... [--apply] [--provisional]
      Weighs each score's takes (take-<n>, engine-<n>, chip-<n>, ay-<n>) and its original (take 0) from their
      notes, prints a table per score and, with --apply, chooses where it is sure. Per take:
        closeness   how close it is to the original's tune, 0 to 1: the best Smith-Waterman
                    local alignment of any of the original's melody lines (each voice's and the
                    top line, ornaments and stray notes dropped, its first 160 notes) with any of
                    the take's (over the seconds that can play, 150 s for a looping score, else
                    1.4 x its length + 2 s), by pitch class under all 12 transpositions with a
                    bonus where the notes' relative lengths agree (so key, octave and tempo do not
                    count), per original note; then as a z against the null (below) and a
                    logistic, 0.5 at z 3. The original is 1.
        quality     (0.3 polyphony + 0.35 harmony + 0.35 rhythm) x the fault multiplier, over the
                    seconds the FM-1 plays of it (60 s with the loop repeated for a looping score,
                    else the score's length): polyphony, the voices sounding at once and whether
                    they differ; harmony, the consonance of the voices sounding together, the
                    key's fit, long minor-second clashes; rhythm, a moving bass under the lead,
                    the drum, a steady pulse
        faults      in words, each lowering the multiplier: refused by the renderer (music-preview
                    exits 3: the pack would play the original), mostly silent, a silent gap of
                    over 2 s, stuck notes, sound in extreme registers, plays only a fragment
                    (shorter than what plays)
        provenance  0 to 1 from the take's source: the game's own period C64 music, a games/ .ay
                    file, a STIL entry naming the Spectrum tune count fully; later covers, demos
                    and remixes little
        score       quality x (0.3 + 0.7 x closeness), closeness raised to 0.6 x provenance (the
                    original's 1): another tune must sound much better to win
      An engine take is the game's own tune read exactly: closeness 1 and provenance 1 by
      construction, and one with no fault (not refused by the renderer, not mostly silent, no
      fragment and so on) is the pick, with high confidence and no flags, ahead of the original
      (a transcription of the same tune) and of every other take (judge.toml says exact = true);
      with a fault it is weighed as any take. Otherwise the highest score is the judge's pick
      (the original can win). Its confidence: low on a
      close call (the pick less than 15% ahead of the next) or when the original is too plain to
      check closeness (itself under z 5) and another take wins, or the pick is refused; medium
      with any flag (the winner not shown to be the same tune, closeness 0.5; a partial melodic
      match, 0.2 to 0.8; fewer than 10 real controls in the null); else high.
      The null: the alignments of 30 takes of other games (another family: an id's "-2", "-3",
      "-ii" and the like left out; none from a file of this score's takes), sampled with the
      score's id as the seed; when the workspace has fewer, other games' originals, then
      controls built from the score itself, copies of the original with each voice's pitches
      shuffled and made-up tunes, alternately (their spread taken as at least 0.4 x their mean,
      as built controls vary less than real music).
      The renderer check runs $FMZX_MUSIC_PREVIEW, else host/target/release/music-preview, for 2 s
      of each take; without it the check is skipped and the judge says so.
      DIR/arrange/<game>/<key>/judge.toml holds the figures: version, score (the score.toml's
      digest, as takes.toml's), plays, search, checkable_z, null_real, null_built, null (the raw
      alignments), renderer, pick, confidence, flags, margin, order (the takes, best first) and a
      [[take]] per take (name, digest of its arranged.toml, closeness, z, raw, provenance,
      quality, polyphony, harmony, rhythm, fault, faults, combined, rank, refused, rendered,
      source). It is reused while the score is the same: a take's figures while its arranged.toml
      and the renderer are, the null while it has 30 real controls or no more are there.
      --provisional marks each null the run makes (null_provisional = true in judge.toml): a
      judge without it does not reuse such a null but makes it again from the workspace's takes
      as they are then (a run with it reuses it as any null). tools/pack-studio judges game by
      game with it while it finds and converts the next games' versions, then runs one judge
      without it over the same games: what that leaves is what one judge over them all, run
      after every game's versions were found, leaves.
      --apply writes the pick to choices.toml for each score the judge is sure of (high) that
      has no choice of the owner's, and marks it in a [judged] table (fmzx-pack reads only
      [choices]); a choice is the judge's while [judged] holds the same value, so the owner's
      choices, made with pick, the page or tools/pack-studio, are never changed. The judge's
      own choices are judged again: changed to another sure pick, or taken back (the score plays
      the original) when it is no longer sure.

  The summary line: generate, engine, chip, ay and judge each end with one line of key=value fields,
  kept stable for tools/pack-studio's jobs view and for scripts (each on one line):
      summary: kind=ai game=<ids|all> scores=N pending=N takes=N model=NAME seconds=S
      summary: kind=engine game=<ids|all> scores=N pending=N takes=N live=N unknown=N failed=N engines=<names|-> seconds=S source=...
      summary: kind=sid game=<ids|all> scores=N pending=N takes=N candidates=N checked=N refused=N best=M seconds=S source=...
      summary: kind=judge game=<ids|all> tunes=N owner=N picked=N applied=N sure=N flagged=N faults=N renderer=checked|skipped seconds=S
  kind is ai (generate), engine (engine), sid (chip), ay (ay) or judge; game the --game ids, comma-separated, or
  all; scores the scores found; pending those worked on (the others had their takes); takes the
  takes written; candidates the (file, subtune) pairs that name the game; checked those
  converted and compared; refused those the converter refused; best the closeness of the take
  written with the best one (two decimals, or - with no take), and source that take's source
  line (absent with no take); seconds the command's time. For engine: takes the engine takes
  written, live the scores a built-in entry already plays exactly, unknown those no known engine
  plays, failed those the reader failed on, engines the engines found (comma-separated), source
  each take's engine and channels ("ntropic, 2 channels + drums", several joined by "; "). For judge: tunes the scores with takes
  judged; owner those with the owner's choice; picked those with the judge's choice; applied
  the choices --apply wrote in this run; sure those without the owner's choice the judge is sure of; flagged
  those with no choice it is not sure of (they need the owner's ear); faults the takes with a
  fault; renderer whether the renderer check ran. source comes last and runs to the end of the
  line; no other value holds a space. A command that stops on an error prints no summary line.

  The progress lines: judge (and generate, engine, chip and ay as they end by judging) say how
  far they are, for tools/pack-studio's bars, each on a line of its own (and engine, before each
  score it reads, "progress: engine <game>/<key>"):
      progress: score <game>/<key> renders=N    before the work: each score with takes, N its
                                                takes the renderer checks (0 without it)
      progress: rendered <game>/<key> <take>    a take checked by the renderer
      [<game>/<key>] judged: <pick> (<confidence>)   a score judged
  A score's share done is (its takes rendered + 1 when judged) / (N + 1).

  tools/arrange-music selftest
      Checks generate (with the rules generator), the files it writes, engine (through a stand-in
      reader: the take on the score's timeline, each channel's voice, more than 3 channels, the
      re-strikes, the drums by kind, a score played exactly live and one with no engine said, the
      cache, the judge's pick and an owner's choice kept, a faulty engine take not picked, pick
      --set and --list), chip (a small collection
      of made-up SID files and a stand-in converter, both written in the temporary folder), ay
      (made-up .ay files, through the same stand-in), the summary lines (a C64 tune found, none
      found by name, songs the converter refuses, scores that have their takes), pick --set and
      --list, the page's save, and the judge: its measures without labels (a transposed copy,
      a faster one in another key and the lead under a band close, an unrelated tune and the
      pitches shuffled not; a full band above its lead alone; a pad a semitone off killing the
      harmony; a stuck note, a long silence, a fragment and a refusal penalised), judge.toml,
      the renderer's refusals (through a stand-in music-preview) and none without it, the
      cache, and --apply on made-up games (sure of a cover, the owner's choices kept, its own
      judged again), in a temporary folder; no network, no model, no Rust build.

Metrics (arranged.toml [metrics], printed by generate and pick --list), for comparing generators:
key and key_r (Krumhansl-Schmuckler estimate from the original's notes, and its correlation);
bass_in_key, pad_in_key (share of the voice's sounding time on the key's scale); bass_consonance,
pad_consonance (share of 10 ms ticks where the voice and the lead sound a unison, third, fourth,
fifth or sixth apart; random notes score about 0.58); on_lead_onsets (share of bass, pad and drum
onsets within 30 ms of a lead onset); lead_nps, bass_nps, pad_nps, drums_nps (notes a second);
generated (notes the generator wrote).

A generator is a function generate_take(score, take_index, rng, settings) -> Take registered with
@generator("name"): score is the parsed score.toml (Score), take_index counts from 1, rng is a
random.Random seeded from the seed, the score and the take, and settings carries the options and a
log function. It returns the notes it wrote (GenNote: seconds from the timeline's start, a GM
program or 128 for drums, a MIDI pitch, and its part, "bass", "chords" or "drums") and a label
for arranged.toml's generator field. The reduction to the FM-1's four voices, the metrics, the
files and the renders are shared.
